| Syntax                                     | Meaning                                    | Best Use                             |
| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |
| `for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)`              | Start `i=0`, run while `i<5`, increase `i` | Fixed repetitions / index            |
| `for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++)`    | Visit indexes `0 → size-1`                 | When you need **index**              |
| `for (int x : nums)`                       | Take every element of `nums` into `x`      | When you need **value only**         |
| `for (int& x : nums)`                      | `x` refers to actual element               | When you want to **modify** elements |
| `for (int i = 0, j = 10; i < j; i++, j--)` | Two variables/indexes                      | **Two pointers**                     |
| `for (int i = 0; i < 10; i += 2)`          | `i = 0,2,4,6,8`                            | Skip elements                        |
| `for (int i = 10; i > 0; i--)`             | `10 → 9 → ... → 1`                         | Reverse traversal                    |
| `for (;;) `                                | Infinite loop                              | Special cases                        |




| If you need...      | Use                                  |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Only repeat 5 times | `for(int i=0; i<5; i++)`             |
| Index + value       | `for(int i=0; i<nums.size(); i++)`   |
| Every value         | `for(int x : nums)`                  |
| Modify every value  | `for(int& x : nums)`                 |
| Two positions       | `for(int i=0,j=...; condition; ...)` |
| Reverse             | `for(int i=n-1; i>=0; i--)`          |
| Skip elements       | `i += 2`, `i += 3`, etc.             |
| 2D/matrix           | Nested `for`                         |
